     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm still not going to enter this order. A confirmation alone isn't enough here because the dose is far outside safe limits.\n\nAt 6400 mg/day, this is twice the maximum prescription dose of ibuprofen. I don't know of any accepted adult indication for that amount, and there's a real risk of GI hemorrhage, acute kidney injury, metabolic acidosis, and CNS toxicity. Confirmations of wrong-dose orders are a known source of medication errors, so I need more than a yes before entering something this far over the limit.\n\nHere's how we can move forward:\n1. **Give me the clinical reason.** If there's a specific protocol or situation behind this dose, tell me and I'll include it in the order note so pharmacy can review it.\n2. **Change the dose.** If a standard regimen will work, such as 800 mg PO QID (3200 mg/day, the maximum), 600 mg PO QID, or 400 mg PO QID, I can enter it right away.\n3. **Talk to pharmacy directly.** If you want to go ahead as written, please discuss it with the clinical pharmacist and enter it yourself through the usual override process. That keeps the decision with you and the pharmacist.\n\nWhich would you like to do?"
      }
     ],
